  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
  A natural deduction presentation of intuitionistic propositional logic has:
</html:p>
    <html:ul><html:li>formulae <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\phi ]]></fr:tex>,<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\psi ]]></fr:tex>,<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\chi ]]></fr:tex> generated by a set of atoms <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p]]></fr:tex>,<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>,<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex>, truth <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\top ]]></fr:tex>, and binary connectives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\land ]]></fr:tex>,<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Rightarrow ]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>judgements <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Phi  \vdash  \phi ]]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Phi ]]></fr:tex> is a finite sequence of formulae <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\phi _1,\phi _2,\ldots ,\phi _n]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>inference rules: structural rules (axiom, weakening, exchange) and logical rules (introduction and elimination rules for each connective)</html:li></html:ul>
  </fr:mainmatter>
